1
00:00:00,165 --> 00:00:01,355
<u>System Design Diagrams</u>

2
00:00:02,681 --> 00:00:04,421
<i>Why use system design diagrams?</i>

3
00:00:05,698 --> 00:00:08,138
A diagram should answer a specific engineering question.

4
00:00:08,838 --> 00:00:11,428
It should expose boundaries, ownership, data flow, ordering,

5
00:00:11,568 --> 00:00:14,278
or failure paths without reproducing every implementation detail.

6
00:00:15,777 --> 00:00:17,617
<i>What is a high-level architecture diagram?</i>

7
00:00:19,126 --> 00:00:22,776
A high-level architecture diagram shows the main system components and their dependencies.

8
00:00:23,486 --> 00:00:27,656
It is useful for discussing boundaries, responsibilities, trust zones, and likely bottlenecks.

9
00:00:29,185 --> 00:00:30,495
<i>What is a component diagram?</i>

10
00:00:32,006 --> 00:00:36,156
A component diagram focuses on major responsibilities and relationships between components.

11
00:00:36,856 --> 00:00:41,446
It should stay at one abstraction level instead of mixing classes, cloud resources, tables, and user journeys.

12
00:00:42,953 --> 00:00:44,223
<i>What is a sequence diagram?</i>

13
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:49,710
A sequence diagram shows the order of interactions between participants for one scenario.

14
00:00:50,410 --> 00:00:54,600
It is especially useful for timeouts, retries, races, acknowledgements, and partial failures.

15
00:00:56,145 --> 00:00:58,605
<i>Why are failure branches useful in sequence diagrams?</i>

16
00:01:00,147 --> 00:01:02,107
A happy-path sequence can hide uncertainty.

17
00:01:02,817 --> 00:01:04,717
Showing a timeout, lost response, retry,

18
00:01:04,897 --> 00:01:08,377
or pending state makes it clear when the system knows an operation succeeded and when it does not.

19
00:01:09,894 --> 00:01:11,214
<i>What is a data-flow diagram?</i>

20
00:01:12,729 --> 00:01:16,569
A data-flow diagram shows where data originates, moves, transforms, and persists.

21
00:01:17,269 --> 00:01:21,579
It helps reason about sources of truth, replicas, privacy, retention, and reconciliation.

22
00:01:23,441 --> 00:01:24,571
<i>What is the C4 model?</i>

23
00:01:25,810 --> 00:01:30,000
The C4 model uses zoom levels: System Context, Container, Component, and Code.

24
00:01:30,690 --> 00:01:33,750
For many system discussions, Context and Container views are enough.

25
00:01:35,265 --> 00:01:36,935
<i>What is a System Context diagram?</i>

26
00:01:38,475 --> 00:01:42,475
A System Context diagram shows users, the system being designed, and external systems.

27
00:01:43,185 --> 00:01:44,995
It defines the broadest useful boundary.

28
00:01:46,513 --> 00:01:48,313
<i>What is a Container diagram in C4?</i>

29
00:01:49,819 --> 00:01:55,029
A Container diagram shows runnable or deployable parts such as a mobile app, API, worker, database, or web app.

30
00:01:55,049 --> 00:01:56,979
“Container” here does not necessarily mean Docker.

31
00:01:58,505 --> 00:01:59,975
<i>When is a state diagram useful?</i>

32
00:02:01,494 --> 00:02:06,274
A state diagram is useful when correctness depends on explicit states and transitions, for example payment, upload,

33
00:02:06,484 --> 00:02:07,694
or synchronization workflows.

34
00:02:09,201 --> 00:02:10,891
<i>When is a class diagram less useful?</i>

35
00:02:12,430 --> 00:02:16,880
A class diagram is less useful when the question is system capacity, network flow, service boundaries,

36
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:18,000
or failure behavior.

37
00:02:18,710 --> 00:02:21,100
Use the diagram type that answers the actual question.

38
00:02:22,633 --> 00:02:24,193
<i>What should arrows on a diagram mean?</i>

39
00:02:25,699 --> 00:02:27,749
Arrows should make direction and semantics clear.

40
00:02:28,459 --> 00:02:33,169
Label them when needed with actions, protocols, events, mutations, acknowledgements, or async boundaries.

41
00:02:34,697 --> 00:02:35,937
<i>What makes a diagram good?</i>

42
00:02:37,475 --> 00:02:42,525
A good diagram has a clear scope, consistent abstraction level, meaningful names, visible durable state and boundaries,

43
00:02:42,675 --> 00:02:44,395
and enough explanation to support a decision.

44
00:02:45,105 --> 00:02:47,395
Outdated diagrams should be updated or removed.
